
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Server-backed, multi-user. That makes notifications worth building.

  Now the core of your actual request: [1m"tasks they care about."[0m That phrase is doing a lot of work, and how we define it
  becomes a table in the schema, so it's worth getting right before anything is written.

  - [1mImplicit interest[0m (you're the assignee, you created it, you commented on it) — zero UI, works from day one, nobody
    has to learn anything. But it's guesswork: it will notify people who stopped caring and miss people who care but
    never touched the task.
  - [1mExplicit watching[0m (a "watch" button, you opt in) — accurate, users control their own noise. Costs a UI affordance
    and a join table, and adoption is usually poor because people forget to click it.
  - [1mBoth[0m (implicit auto-subscribe + explicit watch/unwatch on top) — what GitHub, Jira, and Linear all converged on,
    because each alone fails. Slightly more schema, and you need an explicit "unwatch" state so auto-subscribe can be
    overridden.

  The cheap-to-change-later part is which [3mevents[0m auto-subscribe you. The expensive part is the table shape: if I build
  implicit-only, there's no row to store "I muted this," and adding it later means a migration plus backfill.

  [1mMy recommendation: both[0m, with a [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m table carrying an explicit [38;5;153mmuted[39m state from the start — implicit rules
  write rows into it, the watch button writes rows into it, and mute is just a row. One mechanism, no migration later.
